ed, copy.args = (). That preserves original record untouched. But then record.message for prefix logic should match. Actually if we use copy for super().format, then `record.message` inside format refers to original; prefix computation uses record.message (full) which would mismatch with truncated copy.message. Better: use copy consistently: compute prefix from copy.message.\n\nAlternative simpler: compute `message = record.getMessage()`, truncate if needed, then temporarily... Hmm, the cleanest: \n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = self._truncate_message(message)  # or record.message?\n    if truncated is not message:\n        record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n        record.msg = truncated\n        record.args = ()\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\n\nBut careful: makeLogRecord copies __dict__ including \"message\" key set from previous formatting; logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage() so it's overwritten in the copy. Good.\n\nExc_info in copy is same tuple; formatting exception on copy is fine.\n\nBut wait: the copy is a new LogRecord; doesn't modify original. Good.\n\nAlso keep_full_message: check `record.keep_full_message is True`.\n\nTruncation algorithm: mark = \"...<omitted>...\" len 15. budget N. remaining = N - 15. If message longer than N: head = ceil(remaining/2), tail = remaining - head. result = message[:head] + mark + message[len(message)-tail:].\n\nNote: if remaining could be negative? N>=32 so remaining>=17. Fine.\n\nValidation for max_message_chars: if not None: must be int, not bool, >=32 else ValueError.\n\nAlso need to check that exception/stack_info don't count: yes, since we truncate only message before formatting.\n\nEnviron: new config `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` returning int|None with min 32, error message including name. Descriptor wraps exceptions with name anyway (\"{self.name}: {e}\"). So `int(v)` raising ValueError will be wrapped with name. But validation for <32 must raise ValueError too; wrapping adds name. Note the wrap does `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")` — for ValueError that's fine.\n\nBut be careful: empty string → int(\"\") raises ValueError. Good.\n\nAdd factory method:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int = 0) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value}, must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nHmm, wait: error must contain variable name. The wrapper adds name. But if `raise type(e)(...)` -- ValueError(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") works. Good.\n\nActually careful about the wrapper re-raising: `except TypeError: raise e from None` — only if constructing fails.\n\nAdd config in Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\nLogger default setup: \n```python\nfrom deepinfer import environ as envs  # hmm need import\n```\nCheck how logger.py imports envs elsewhere? Let me check whole logger.py for \"environ\". I haven't seen it. I'll add `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. But careful about import cycles: environ imports os etc only, no logger. Safe.\n\nIn _setup_logger: \n```python\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\nWait: \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器；已创建的旧格式化器保留原预算。\" Reading envs attr at setup time gives cached value. Good. `_setup_logger` is called at import — that reads env var at import; fine.\n\nBut hmm - test may call `logger._setup_logger()` after rearm. Reading envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS - attribute access. If it's cached attribute in envs.__dict__, fine.\n\n